"Rast" <rast2@hotmail.com> wrote:

> If a challenging conduct doesn't push your score
> upwards, why should anything else?  Score is
> supposed to measure how well the player did;
> otherwise it is meaningless.

When I go hunting, say for elk, and I bring home and
mount an elk head on my wall, my "score" is how many
"points" there are on that large deer's antlers.

When I tell people I shot it with an elephant gun
from a tree mounted hunting blind over the only
watering hole for fifty kilometers around (pudding
farming), my bragging rights go down, my raw score
is discounted in the onlooker's mind.

When, oppositely, I tell them I felled the animal
with a yew bow and a clothyard arrow from a stealthy
approach on foot (wishless atheist), my bragging
rights go up, each "point" is worth quite a bit
more.

If I tell them I killed the animal with an obsidian
knife with a rawhide wrapped handle as my only
weapon (Zen wishless atheist foodless), I will be
declared "god of the hunt" (and probably sacrificed
at the next Beltaine for my troubles), and no one
will care how many points the antlers have.

The hunter stalking elk afoot with a knife isn't
playing the same game as the tree perched hunter
with the 70 caliber rifle, and doesn't, probably,
ever bother to notice how many "points" the elk's
antlers tally. S/he's playing some other game
entirely, because mere "meat hunting" has lost its
challenge.

You're never going to develop a scoring system that
works for both attitudes. Conduct games are about
bragging rights, they are profoundly _not about_
points on the score table, and trying to force them
into that mold will only cheapen conduct play into
something _everyone_ is expected to do.

Where's the bragging potential in common behaviors?

Don't go there. Instead, just annotate the high
score table also with the conducts kept, so the
right to brag is documented, and (maybe) implement
code to keep track of many more conducts(*).

xanthian.

